<speak>
<s>From <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news, this is your briefing for <say-as interpret-as="date" format="dmy">16032026</say-as>.</s>
<break time="1s"/>
<s>A fragment of an Iranian missile struck the United States consul general's residence in Jerusalem — the first time Iranian ordnance has landed on a site carrying American sovereign status under international law.</s> <s>President Trump has confirmed that every Israeli missile hitting Iran is American: United States planes, missiles, and Patriot batteries are all instruments of Israel's campaign.</s> <s>Washington has not said whether it considers the strike an attack on American sovereignty, and that unanswered question may be the most consequential open matter in the conflict today.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In politics.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Eight hundred thousand people have fled their homes in Lebanon in just ten days, as Israeli strikes have killed 826 people since the second of March — 106 of them children — the country's second mass displacement in six months.</s> <s>Lebanon's health system is near collapse, and no ceasefire talks are scheduled.</s> <s>In Gaza and the West Bank, Israeli forces killed nine police officers in a single vehicle on Sunday, bringing the Palestinian death toll since October's ceasefire to 670, with no United Nations Security Council session convened on Gaza since the wider Iran war began.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Japan imports ninety percent of its oil through the Strait of Hormuz but will not send warships there.</s> <s>Its pacifist constitution bars the military from joining others' armed conflicts, and Australia has also refused, leaving the United States without a single Pacific ally for its proposed escort operation.</s> <s>Five ships now pass through Hormuz each day against a pre-war average of 138 — an oil shock Tokyo cannot address by force.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Thousands defied a United Kingdom government ban to rally in London against United States and Israeli strikes, gathering in numbers the authorities could not suppress despite prohibiting the annual <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="æl kʊdz">Al-Quds</phoneme> Day march.</s> <s>The government officially refuses to join the war, but its ban on those who oppose it has done more to expose that contradiction than resolve it.</s> <s>No explanation has been offered for which future demonstrations will be permitted.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Malaysian police have questioned activists under a law carrying a twenty-year prison term, at the direction of a prime minister who himself spent six years in prison on charges he called politically fabricated.</s> <s>Anwar Ibrahim, once internationally recognized as a prisoner of conscience, now faces credible accusations of deploying the same legal machinery against critics, students, and academics who challenge his government.</s> <s>Civil society groups are demanding the government specify which forms of dissent it considers a threat to the state.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>On the economy.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>South Korea's won breached 1,500 to the dollar on Monday — its lowest level in seventeen years — as the Hormuz blockade drives up import costs for a country that sources seventy percent of its oil through that strait.</s> <s>A widening trade deficit has pulled the currency lower even as the central bank holds rates steady.</s> <s>The finance minister warned of possible market intervention but named no trigger level.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>The Asian Development Bank has given Pakistan until the fifth of April to award contracts on a three-hundred-and-sixty-million-dollar road loan, or lose the funding entirely.</s> <s>A highway connecting Pakistan to Central Asia, the project has missed its contracting deadlines for over a year — a symptom of institutional strain on a country simultaneously managing war fuel costs, Ramadan energy shortages, and International Monetary Fund conditionality.</s> <s>No further extensions are possible, the bank has warned.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Beijing told Washington to correct its erroneous ways after the United States launched two new trade investigations targeting Chinese exports last week — the latest confrontation following February's Supreme Court ruling that struck down President Trump's tariffs as unconstitutional.</s> <s>Simultaneously, Western retailers are retreating from China's consumer market: Guess is closing all its mainland stores this month, joining Old Navy and four <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ɪndɪtɛks">Inditex</phoneme> labels in a withdrawal driven by Chinese consumers shifting to domestic brands.</s> <s>A Trump-Xi summit is scheduled for the thirty-first of March, though Trump has warned the Middle East war may delay it.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In science.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>The war in Gaza has already generated thirty-three million tonnes of carbon dioxide — one-tenth of what Kuwait's burning oil wells released across all of 1991.</s> <s>United States and Israeli strikes are now targeting Hormuz refineries and oil tankers, adding to an environmental toll that no international treaty requires any state to measure, report, or remediate.</s> <s>Military carbon emissions are the only significant category exempt from every climate accounting framework now in force.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s><phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ŋoʊ baʊ tʃaʊ">Ngô Bảo Châu</phoneme>, Vietnam's first winner of the Fields Medal, is leaving the University of Chicago for the University of Hong Kong after fifteen years, citing things about the United States he no longer wishes to accept.</s> <s>His departure is the highest-profile individual case of what researchers are calling a Trump-era brain drain from American universities.</s> <s>He plans to establish a mathematics research center in Hong Kong — a quiet act of institution-building in a climate growing more hostile elsewhere.</s>
<break time="900ms"/>
<s>In technology.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Institutional crypto lender BlockFills has filed for bankruptcy after losing seventy-five million dollars, with a court freezing its assets following customer allegations that funds were misappropriated rather than held in segregated accounts.</s> <s>The company reports assets of fifty to one hundred million dollars against liabilities reaching up to five hundred million — built on the same absent fund-segregation rules that brought down FTX.</s> <s>Three years after that collapse, the industry has not closed this structural gap.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Bitcoin has risen thirteen percent since the Iran war began, with United States spot exchange-traded funds drawing one-point-three billion dollars in March inflows as the currency tests its role as a crisis store of value.</s> <s>Its fifty-day moving average was crossed for the first time since January.</s> <s>Large short positions cluster near the seventy-five-thousand-dollar level, and how Bitcoin behaves there will test whether the digital gold thesis holds under real geopolitical pressure.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Canada's parliament is reviewing a bill that would require every telecommunications company to build permanent surveillance interception infrastructure into their networks.</s> <s>Bill C-22 replaces some warrantless access to subscriber data with court orders, but mandates that the capacity to intercept communications remain permanently embedded across every network in the country.</s> <s>Critics say the bill trades one civil liberties problem for a more durable one.</s>
<break time="1s"/>
<s>That's your briefing from <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news.</s>
</speak>